---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide>
  <h3><span class="main-gradient">Let's go further</span> ᯓ🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️</h3>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript"
    >self.onmessage = (event) => {};</code
  >
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript mid-dimension"
    >self.onmessage = (event) => {};</code
  >
  <ul style="margin-block-start: 0.5em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">📌 data</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">📌 origin</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">📌 source</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">📌 ports</li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p><b is:raw>-->all of these properties are read-only<--</b></p>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        data: The data sent from the main thread to the web worker
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        origin: Indicates the origin of the message, i.e., the URL of the
        context that sent the message
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        source: Represents the context that sent the message. In the case of Web
        Workers, this property is usually null because messages come from the
        main thread or other Workers, not from windows or iframes
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        ports: An array of MessagePort objects associated with the message.
        MessagePort are used for advanced two-way communication between threads
      </li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript"
    >postMessage(message, transfer)</code
  >
</p-slide>
<p-slide class="center">
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript mid-dimension"
    >postMessage(message, transfer)</code
  >
  <div
    class="flex two-columns-divided-vertically"
    style="margin-block-start: 1em"
  >
    <div>
      <p>💬 <span class="main-sub-gradient">message</span></p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="from left">
        📨 will be in the data field in the message object
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom">🚩 required</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>🚚 <span class="main-gradient">transfer</span></p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="from right">
        🚌 only transferable objects like ArrayBuffer or ImageBitmap
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom">😶‍🌫️ optional</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      By default, when you use postMessage(message), the message is copied into
      the Worker's context (or the main thread) by structured cloning mechanism.
      But if you want to improve performance, you can transfer specific types of
      data instead of copying them
    </p>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li>ArrayBuffer</li>
      <li>ImageBitmap</li>
      <li>OffscreenCanvas</li>
      <li>ReadableStream, WritableStream, TransformStream</li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript"
    >worker.onerror = (event) => {};</code
  >
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <code style="width: fit-content" class="language-javascript mid-dimension"
    >worker.onerror = (event) => {};</code
  >
  <ul class="space" style="margin-block-start: 0.7em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">⛔ ErrorEvent</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      💬 Optional string on <span class="main-sub-gradient">message</span> properties
      <pre
        style="margin-block-start: 1em; border-radius: 0.5em; box-shadow: inset 0 0 0.09em rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25)"><code is:raw class="language-javascript">worker.onerror = (event) => {
  console.log(event.message);
};</code></pre>
    </li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    🤝🏻 <span class="main-gradient">Web Worker - Practical Use Cases</span>
  </h3>
  <ul style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.1em" class="space mid-dimension">
    <li>🖼️ Image compression and processing</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">🧾 CSV data processing and formatting</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">🧮 Complex algorithms</li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3><span class="main-gradient">Let's go further</span> ᯓᯓ🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️</h3>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h2>🤝🏻 <span class="main-gradient">Shared Worker</span></h2>
  <PNotes>
    Dedicated Workers are the most common: They are created from a single page
    with new Worker(). Each instance is independent (if you open two tabs of the
    same app, you will have two distinct Web Workers). Perfect for operations
    tied only to the current page (e.g., file parsing or heavy calculations).
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>🤝🏻 <span class="main-gradient">Shared Worker</span></h3>
  <ul style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.2em" class="space mid-dimension">
    <li>
      🌍 <span class="main-sub-gradient">Can be shared</span> across multiple tabs
      of the same origin
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      😎 <span class="main-sub-gradient">Keep alive</span> until all tabs are closed
      (even if the page is reloaded)
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">
      💬 Communicate through the <span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >MessagePort API</span
      >
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      MessagePort API:
      https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MessagePort : similar to
      postMessage but more powerful and flexible
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    🤝🏻 <span class="main-gradient">Shared Worker - Practical Use Cases</span>
  </h3>
  <ul style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.1em" class="space mid-dimension">
    <li>
      🗣️ Cross-tab communication through the <span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >MessagePort API</span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">🗃️ Shared Cache or Data Storage</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">👥 Session Management</li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">Let&#39;s Code 👨‍💻</h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="shared-worker.js">
 <code is:raw class="language-javascript" style="font-size: 0.76em;"> 
    const clients = [];
    
    self.onconnect = (event) => {
      const [port] = event.ports;
      clients.push(port);
      port.postMessage("Worker connected");
      port.onmessage = (e) => {
        clients.forEach((client) =>
          client.postMessage(
            `New client connected with message: "${e.data}"`
          )
        );
      };
    };</code>
</pre>
  <p-fragment
    ><span
      style="--circle-sketch-height: 1.4em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 8.3em; width: 8em; inset-inline-start: 1.9em; z-index: 1"
      class="circle-sketch-highlight"></span></p-fragment
  >
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">Let&#39;s Code 👨‍💻</h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line language-javascript"
    data-label-file="main.js">
    <code is:raw class="language-javascript">
    const sharedWorker = new SharedWorker("shared-worker.js");
    sharedWorker.port.start();

    sharedWorker.port.onmessage = (e) => {
      console.log(e.data);
    };

    sharedWorker.port.postMessage("Hello from Tab 1");</code>
  </pre>
  <p-fragment
    ><span
      style="--circle-sketch-height: 1.8em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 8.3em; width: 5em; inset-inline-start: 5.9em; z-index: 1"
      class="circle-sketch-highlight"></span></p-fragment
  >
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h2>⛓️‍💥 <span class="main-gradient">when client disconnect</span></h2>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">Let&#39;s Code 👨‍💻</h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line language-javascript"
    data-label-file="main.js">
    <code is:raw class="language-javascript" style="font-size: 0.92em;">
  const clients = [];
  self.onconnect = (event) => {
    // ...other stuff
    port.onclose = () => {
      clients.splice(clients.indexOf(port), 1);
      clients.forEach((client) =>
        client.postMessage(
          `Client disconnected. Total clients remaining: ${clients.length}`
        )
      );
    };
  }</code></pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      When the client disconnects (closing the tab or window), the onclose event
      is triggered.
    </p>
    <p>
      The worker removes the client from the list and broadcasts a "Client
      disconnected" message to all remaining clients.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide class="center">
  <h2><span class="main-gradient">Web Worker limitations</span> ❌</h2>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <ul class="space" style="line-height: 1em">
    <li>❌ <span class="main-sub-gradient">No access to the DOM</span></li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ❌
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >No access to global variables like <code style="font-size: 0.8em"
          >window</code
        >, <code style="font-size: 0.8em">document</code>, or
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">parent</code></span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ❌
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"
        >No synchronous APIs like <code style="font-size: 0.8em">alert()</code
        >, <code style="font-size: 0.8em">confirm()</code>, or
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">prompt()</code></span
      >
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      ❌ <span class="main-sub-gradient">No access to storage APIs</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li>
        ❌ Some APIs like localStorage, sessionStorage, and cookies are not
        accessible. IndexedDB is only partially available.
      </li>
      <li>
        ❌ No synchronous import of ES modules - Modules must be imported
        explicitly and asynchronously (importScripts() works only in classic
        workers, not module workers).
      </li>
      <li>
        ❌ Restricted access to certain JavaScript APIs - Web Workers cannot use
        WebRTC, CanvasRenderingContext2D (except OffscreenCanvas), some
        security-related APIs, and other browser-specific features.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<style lang="scss">
  .two-columns-divided-vertically {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-content: center;
    align-items: center;
  }
</style>
